    "case_summary": "A man in his 40s presented with a slowly growing tumor on the scalp. The patient was otherwise healthy with no significant medical history.\n\nSkin examination revealed an 8-mm red, firm, mobile nodule on the left temporal scalp. Dermoscopic evaluation showed irregular red lacunae with irregular vessels and absence of septa.\n\n{'Imaging Studies': {'Dermoscopy': {'Image Title': 'Dermoscopic findings of scalp nodule', 'Image Description': '8-mm red nodule showing irregular red lacunae without septa and irregular linear vessels'}}, 'Pathological Studies': {'Histopathology': {'H&E Staining': 'Well-circumscribed dermal vascular tumor extending to the superficial subcutaneous tissue, composed of ectatic thin-walled vessels with multiple intravascular branching papillary structures. Structures covered by swollen endothelial cells with numerous intracytoplasmic eosinophilic hyaline globules.', 'Special Stains': 'Periodic acid−Schiff (PAS) staining showed positive hyaline globules in the cytoplasm of endothelial cells', 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Tumor vessels were positive for CD-31, negative for D2-40 (podoplanin) and HHV-8. α-smooth muscle actin highlighted the pericytes in the papillae. Basal membrane and stroma inside the papillae were positive for collagen IV.'}}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Pyogenic granuloma\nB. Papillary hemangioma\nC. Hobnail hemangioma\nD. Hobnail Hemangioma (Targetoid Hemosiderotic Hemangioma)\n",
